However, scarcity of the minerals has prevented the measurement of their properties. Moreover, while it was reported that ZH and ST undergo reversible loss of guest water molecules,11 there has been no evidence that such dehydration proceeds with retention of the hcb-networks, which would be expected in a functional MOF material.14
We now show that ZH and ST can indeed exhibit functional behavior on par with known oxalate MOF proton conductors. Specifically, synthetic mineral analogues exhibit high proton conductivities at room temperature, undergo thermal removal of included water guests without changes to the underlying hcb-networks and, by using solution calorimetry, we demonstrate they are also of high thermodynamic stability.
## Experimental
All reagents were available commercially and were used without additional purification. Oxalic acid, NaOH, ${\mathsf{N a H C O}}_{3},$ $\mathsf{M g O}$ , ${\mathsf{M g C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were obtained from Sigma Aldrich.
Synthesis of ST1 and ST2 was performed by dissolving FeO(OH) (first obtained by mixing of aqueous solutions of $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and $N a H C O_{3},$ followed by filtration) in an aqueous solution of oxalic acid. After addition of NaOH and ${\mathsf{M g C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0.$ the solution was filtered and placed in a refrigerator at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for crystallization and slow evaporation. The resulting ST1 and ST2 crystals were separated by hand, based on crystal morphology. Slow evaporation led to mixtures containing ST1 as the major product, while faster evaporation, e.g. in a rotary evaporator, gave ST2 as the major product.
Synthesis of ZH was performed in two steps. In the first step, $\mathsf{N a M g A l}(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})_{3}{\times}9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ was obtained by dissolving aluminum metal in an aqueous solution of oxalic acid with sonication, followed by addition of $N a O H$ , ${\mathsf{M g C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and filtration. Evaporation of the liquid led to crystallization of $\mathsf{N a M g A l(C_{2}O_{4})_{3}{\times}9H_{2}O},$ isolated by filtration. In the second step, equivalent amounts of $\mathsf{N a M g A l}(\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{9}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and NaMgFe $(C_{2}O_{4})_{3}\cdot9H_{2}O$ (synthesized as described for ST1 and ST2 above) were dissolved in a small amount of water. After several days at $4\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ yellow-green needles of ZH were isolated. Bulk ZH sample was obtained by milling equimolar amounts of $\mathsf{N a M g A l}(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{9H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and NaMgFe $(C_{2}O_{4})_{3}\cdot9H_{2}O$ with $40~{\upmu\updownarrow}~{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon® jars with one zirconia ball $10~\mathsf{m m}$ diameter) at $25\mathsf{H z}$ .
Dehydration of ST1 and ST2 single crystals was performed with gentle heating at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under vacuum over a period of 12 hours. The compounds are further denoted as ST1d and ST2d denoting dehydrated ST1 and ST2 respectively.
Powder X-ray Diffraction (PXRD) data for rehydration experiments was collected on a PROTO AXRD benchtop instrument equipped with a DECTRIS MYTHEN2R 1D detector, using nickel-filtered $\mathsf{C u K}_{\alpha}\left(\lambda{=}0.154056\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\right)$ radiation. Rehydration was performed in a custom-made sealed sample holder15 kept at $98\%$ relative humidity with the aid of a saturaVtiewdAraticqleueOnoliunes solution of ${\sf K}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ . DOI: 10.1039/C8SC05088K
Variable temperature (VT) dehydration experiments and verification after impendance measurements were performed on a Bruker D8 Advance instrument equipped with a LYNXEYE XE-T detector using nickel-filtered $\mathsf{C u}K_{\upalpha}$ $(\lambda~=~0.154056~\lambda$ ) radiation. The setup was equipped with Anton Paar CHC plus+ chamber. Diffractograms were collected in a stepwise fashion in a dry environment. After each collection (ca. 15 min), temperature in the chamber was raised by $\mathsf{1^{\circ}C}$ at a rate of 1 $\mathsf{{^o C/}m i n}$ and a new collection started.
Crystal Structures of ST2, ST1d and ST2d were determined by single crystal X-ray diffraction using a Bruker D8 APEX2 X-ray diffractometer and graphite-monochromated MoKα $(\lambda{=}0.71073\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ radiation. Structures were solved by intrinsic phasing in SHELXT16 and refined on F2 using SHELXL.17 Wherever possible, hydrogen atoms participating in hydrogen bonds were located from the electron density map. Crystallographic data in CIF format has been deposited with the Cambridge Structural Database (CSD), CCDC 1868681-1868685.
Fourier-transform infrared attenuated total reflectance (FTIR-ATR) studies were done on a Bruker VERTEX 70 instrument with a PLATINUM diamond crystal ATR unit.
Thermogravimetric analysis and differential scanning calorimetry (TGA and DSC) analysis was done on a MettlerToledo TGA DSC 1 Star system thermobalance using alumina crucibles under a stream of nitrogen $(50\mathsf{m l}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}.$ ) and a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ from $25^{\circ}C$ until $200^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and in a stream of air $(50\mathsf{m l}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ at a heating rate of $10\phantom{0}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ from $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ to 700 $^\circ C$ . Sample size was between $2\mathsf{m g}$ and $10~\mathsf{m g}$ .
Deuterated ST1 and ZH were prepared by first fully dehydrating the parent phases NaMgFe $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})_{3}\times9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (ST1) and $\mathsf{N a M g A l}(\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})_{3}{\times}9\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ under vacuum at $70\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ after which the materials were dissolved in ${\mathsf{D}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and recrystallized in a dry $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Deuteration was confirmed by FTIR-ATR, and the identity of samples was verified by PXRD.
Alternative current (AC) impedance measurement was performed by the conventional two-probe method. Samples (ST1, ST2, and ZH1) were pelletized about $\sim1.2\mathsf{m m}$ in thickness and $2.5\mathsf{m m}$ in diameter. The pellet was connected to electrode with gold paste and gold wires ( $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in diameter). The test is performed in a temperature- and humidity-controlled chamber, which was connected to a Solartron SI 1260 Impedance/ GainPhase Analyzer and 1296 Dielectric Interface. The impedance measurement was executed in the frequency range from ${\mathsf{1}}{\mathsf{H z}}$ to $10\ M\ H z$ at $298~\mathsf{K}$ Proton conductivity and activation energy barrier were estimated by Eq. (1) and (2), where σ is the conductivity $(\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}).$ , $\iota$ is the measured sample thickness (cm), $s$ is the electrode area $({\mathsf{c m}}^{2})$ and $z$ is the impedance value. In Eq (2), A is a pre-exponential factor, $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, and $\boldsymbol{E}\mathsf{a}$ is the activation energy of ionic conduction.
